\frametitle{A package-style example in \texttt {R}}
For two-stage randomized designs with interference, \texttt{RCT2} and \texttt{latenetwork} provide useful starting points.

\begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
\begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
\begin{lstlisting}
# RCT2: two-stage randomized designs
library(RCT2)

# see package vignette for the india
# insurance example
# rand <- CADErand(india, 0.95)
\end{lstlisting}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.48\textwidth}
\begin{lstlisting}
# latenetwork: unknown interference
# with IV-style estimands
library(latenetwork)

# data <- datageneration(n = 2000)
# result <- direct(Y=data$Y, D=data$D,
#                  Z=data$Z, A=data$A,
#                  S=rep(TRUE, 2000))
\end{lstlisting}
\end{column}
\end{columns}

\begin{alertblock}{Interpretation}
These packages are useful anchors, but the main conceptual work is still in specifying the network and the exposure mapping.
\end{alertblock}
